---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | salarynegotiationcalc.com';
const description = 'Contact the salarynegotiationcalc.com editorial team with corrections, feedback, or questions about salary negotiation content.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact salarynegotiationcalc.com</h1>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We're a small team, so email is the only channel we monitor &mdash; there's no live chat or phone line, and that's by design: a real negotiation question deserves a considered answer, not a rushed one.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections to industry multipliers, location premiums, or citations.</strong> Our figures are sourced to BLS Occupational Employment Statistics and commercial salary database research that gets updated periodically. If a page cites a multiplier or premium that's out of date, tell us which page and what the correct figure is; we verify against the primary source before making any change.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> If a sentence in a guide is ambiguous, contradicts itself, or is just hard to follow, we want to know &mdash; plain language is one of our core editorial standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new content.</strong> If you searched for a negotiation question and couldn't find it on the Site &mdash; a specific industry, a competing-offer scenario, a total-compensation component the calculator doesn't handle &mdash; let us know what you were trying to figure out.</li>
    <li><strong>Press and business inquiries.</strong> Journalists covering compensation trends or the labor market, and businesses interested in advertising, can reach the same address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>We read every message. Corrections and editorial questions typically get a reply within about five business days. If a correction is verified, we update the affected page and note the date of the material change, consistent with our <a href="/editorial/">corrections policy</a>. We don't always reply to content suggestions individually, but we do track them for future guides.</p>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't tell you whether to accept a specific offer, what to ask for at a specific company, or whether your current pay is fair for your exact situation &mdash; that's personalized career advice, and it depends on details a calculator and a short email can't capture. We also don't recommend specific recruiters, coaches, or employers, and we don't accept payment to feature or promote anyone on the Site. If you email us asking for offer-specific guidance, we'll point you back to this policy and to the resources below.</p>

  <h2>Before you email</h2>
  <p>Many questions we get are already answered on the Site: check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions on salary history laws, competing offers, and total compensation components, or the <a href="/guides/">guides</a> &mdash; including <a href="/guides/how-to-negotiate-salary/">how to negotiate salary</a>, <a href="/guides/salary-research-guide/">researching your market rate</a>, <a href="/guides/negotiating-job-offers/">negotiating job offers</a>, and <a href="/guides/salary-negotiation-misconceptions/">common salary negotiation misconceptions</a>. If your question is about how the calculator arrived at a number, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> walks through the multipliers and adjustments in detail.</p>

  <h2>Your next step</h2>
  <p>This site is a starting point, not a substitute for someone who knows your specific market and situation. If you want a second opinion before a real conversation, a career coach can help you rehearse the conversation itself and read the room on company-specific dynamics, and a recruiter who works in your industry often has real-time visibility into what similar candidates are actually being offered right now &mdash; visibility that published survey data lags behind. If you'd rather validate the numbers yourself first, cross-reference this calculator's estimate against live listings on Glassdoor, Levels.fyi (especially useful in tech), or LinkedIn Salary, since real postings pick up market shifts faster than annual surveys do. One exception: if your situation involves a pay gap you believe is tied to a protected characteristic &mdash; gender, race, age, disability, or similar &mdash; that's no longer just a negotiation question, and it's worth talking to an employment attorney or your state's fair employment agency about a potential equal-pay or discrimination claim rather than relying on this calculator alone.</p>
</BaseLayout>
